<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<subsection xml:id="ch7-section-thermodynamics-review-subsection-stability" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Static stability</title>

    <p>
        Differentiating the definition of potential temperature
        <xref ref="eqn-potential-temperature" text="type-global" />
        with respect to <m>z</m> produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
                \amp =
                \frac{\partial}{\partial z}
                \left(
                    T \left(
                    \frac{p_0}{p}
                    \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \right)
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{p_0}{p}
                \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                +
                \frac{R_d}{c_p} T
                \left(
                    \frac{p_0}{p}
                \right)^{\frac{R_d}{c_p}-1}
                \left(
                    - \frac{p_0}{p^2}
                \right)
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \amp =
                \left(
                    \frac{p_0}{p}
                \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                -
                \frac{R_d}{c_p} T
                \left(
                    \frac{p_0}{p}
                \right)^\frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{1}{p}
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-stability-start" number="yes">
                \amp =
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                -
                \frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\theta}{p}
                \frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Assuming the atmosphere is in hydrostatic balance,
        <xref ref="eqn-stability-start" text="type-global" /> can be written
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
                \amp =
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                -
                \frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\theta}{p}
                (-\rho g)
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-stability-middle" number="yes">
                \amp =
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                +
                \frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\theta}{p}
                \rho g
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Substituting the ideal gas law <xref ref="eqn-ideal-gas-law-density" text="type-global" />
        into <xref ref="eqn-stability-middle" text="type-global" /> produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md>
            <mrow>
                \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
                \amp =
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                +
                \frac{R_d}{c_p}
                \frac{\theta}{p}
                \left(
                    \frac{p}{R_dT}
                \right)
                g
            </mrow>
            <mrow>
                \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
                \amp =
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z}
                +
                \frac{\theta}{T}
                \frac{g}{c_p}
            </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eqn-stability-end" number="yes">
                \frac{T}{\theta}  \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
                \amp =
                \frac{\partial T}{\partial z} + \frac{g}{c_p}
            </mrow>
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        Finally,
        substituting the definitions of the environmental lapse rate <xref ref="eqn-environmental-lapse-rate" text="type-global" />
        and the DALR <xref ref="eqn-dry-adiabatic-lapse-rate" text="type-global" />
        into <xref ref="eqn-stability-end" text="type-global" /> produces
    </p>

    <p>
        <md xml:id="eqn-stability" number="yes">
            \frac{T}{\theta}
            \frac{\partial \theta}{\partial z}
            =
            \Gamma_d - \Gamma
        </md>
    </p>

    <p>
        This leads to three cases for <term>local stability</term>,
        which according to <xref ref="petty" text="custom">Petty (2008)</xref>
        is the stability of atmospheric layers to small vertical displacements of air parcels within them.
        The term "local" is used to emphasize that air parcel vertical displacements are small
        and so only information about the local environmental lapse rate is needed.
    </p>

    <p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When <m>\Gamma_d \gt \Gamma</m>
                    (i.e., when a perfectly dry air parcel rising adiabatically cools faster with increasing height than its environment),
                    <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} \gt 0</m>.
                    The air parcel is negatively buoyant,
                    and thus <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} \gt 0</m>
                    is a statement of <term>static stability</term>.
                    In other words,
                    the layer of air surrounding the air parcel is <term>stably stratified</term>.
                    In this type of layer,
                    the air parcel will tend to return to its original location if slightly disturbed.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When <m>\Gamma_d \lt \Gamma</m>
                    (i.e., when a dry air parcel rising adiabatically cools slower with increasing height than its environment),
                    <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} \lt 0</m>.
                    The air parcel is positively buoyant,
                    and thus <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} \lt 0</m>
                    is a statement of <term>static instability</term>.
                    In other words,
                    the layer of air surrounding the air parcel is <term>unstably stratified</term>.
                    In this type of layer,
                    the air parcel will convect freely away from its original location.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    When <m>\Gamma_d = \Gamma</m>
                    (i.e., when a dry air parcel rising adiabatically cools at the same rate with increasing height as its environment),
                    <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} = 0</m>.
                    The air parcel is neutrally buoyant,
                    and thus <m>\dfrac{\partial \theta}{\partial z} = 0</m>
                    is a statement of <term>static neutrality</term>.
                    In other words,
                    the layer of air is <term>neutrally stratified</term>.
                    In this type of layer,
                    the air parcel will move to wherever it is displaced and remain there,
                    or it will continue moving with constant speed in the direction of displacement.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </p>

</subsection>